<ImageAcquisitionInfo>
	<Help>
	   	<AdaptorHelp property="Brightness">Specify the lightness or darkness of an image using relative values.

Brightness controls the black level of an image. When brightness is controlled 
using relative values, the unit for brightness is device specific. For more 
information about controlling the brightness feature of a particular camera, 
see the documentation that came with the device.

Depending on the acquisition device, this property may have an associated 
BrightnessMode, BrightnessAbsolute, or BrightnessControl property.

Setting the Brightness property will set the BrightnessMode property, if 
available, to 'manual' and the BrightnessControl property, if available, to 
'relative'.

<SeeAlso>BrightnessAbsolute, BrightnessControl, BrightnessMode.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>
	   
	   	<AdaptorHelp property="BrightnessMode">Specify whether the Brightness property is controlled manually or automatically.

If specified as 'auto', the device manages brightness. If specified as 
'manual', you can control brightness using the Brightness or the 
BrightnessAbsolute property, if available.

The Brightness, BrightnessAbsolute, BrightnessControl, and BrightnessMode 
properties are closely related. When you specify a value for either the 
Brightness or BrightnessAbsolute property, the BrightnessMode property is 
automatically set to 'manual'. 

If you change the BrightnessMode property from 'auto' to 'manual', the 
Brightness property is automatically set back to the value stored by that 
property if the BrightnessControl value is 'relative'. Otherwise, the 
BrightnessAbsolute property is automatically set back to the value stored 
by that property if the BrightnessControl value is 'absolute'.

<SeeAlso>Brightness, BrightnessAbsolute, BrightnessControl.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>

	   	<AdaptorHelp property="BrightnessAbsolute">Specify the lightness or darkness of an image using absolute values.
	   	
BrightnessAbsolute controls lightness or darkness by specifying the black 
level offset. You specify the value as a percentage. For more information 
about controlling the brightness feature of a particular camera, see the 
documentation that came with the device.

Depending on the acquisition device, this property may have an associated mode 
property allowing this value to be controlled automatically by the device or 
for it to be manually configured.

Setting the BrightnessAbsolute property will set the BrightnessMode property, 
if available, to 'manual' and the BrightnessControl property to 'absolute'.

<SeeAlso>Brightness, BrightnessMode, BrightnessControl.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>

	   	<AdaptorHelp property="BrightnessControl">Specify whether the brightness feature is controlled by relative or absolute values.
	   	
If specified as 'relative', brightness is controlled by the value specified by 
the Brightness property. If specified as 'absolute', brightness is controlled 
by the value specified by the BrightnessAbsolute property.

<SeeAlso>Brightness.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>   	
	   		   	
	   	<AdaptorHelp property="AutoExposure">Specify the exposure using relative values.
	   	
AutoExposure specifies the exposure value. When exposure is controlled using 
relative values, the unit for exposure is device specific. For more 
information about controlling the exposure feature of a particular camera, see 
the documentation that came with the device.

Depending on the acquisition device, this property may have an associated 
AutoExposureMode, AutoExposureAbsolute, or AutoExposureControl property.

Setting the AutoExposure property will set the AutoExposureMode property, if 
available, to 'manual' and the AutoExposureControl property, if available, to 
'relative'.

<SeeAlso>AutoExposureAbsolute, AutoExposureControl, AutoExposureMode.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>
	
	   	<AdaptorHelp property="AutoExposureMode">Specify whether the AutoExposure property is controlled manually or automatically.

If specified as 'auto', the device manages the exposure setting. If specified 
as 'manual', you can control exposure using the AutoExposure or the 
AutoExposureAbsolute property, if available.

The AutoExposure, AutoExposureAbsolute, AutoExposureControl, and 
AutoExposureMode properties are closely related. When you specify a value for 
either the AutoExposure or AutoExposureAbsolute property, the AutoExposureMode 
property is automatically set to 'manual'.

If you change the AutoExposureMode property from 'auto' to 'manual', the 
AutoExposure property is automatically set back to the value stored by that 
property if the AutoExposureControl value is 'relative'. Otherwise, the 
AutoExposureAbsolute property is automatically set back to the value stored by 
that property if the AutoExposureControl value is 'absolute'.

<SeeAlso>AutoExposure, AutoExposureAbsolute, AutoExposureControl.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>

	   	<AdaptorHelp property="AutoExposureAbsolute">Specify the exposure using absolute values.
	   	
AutoExposureAbsolute specifies the exposure value (EV). An EV number defines 
certain combinations of aperture and shutter speed settings that determine 
exposure. For more information about controlling the AutoExposure feature of a 
particular camera, see the documentation that came with the device.

Depending on the acquisition device, this property may have an associated mode 
property allowing this value to be controlled automatically by the device or 
for it to be manually configured.

Setting the AutoExposureAbsolute property will set the AutoExposureMode 
property, if available, to 'manual' and the AutoExposureControl property to 
'absolute'.

<SeeAlso>AutoExposure, AutoExposureMode, AutoExposureControl.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>

	   	<AdaptorHelp property="AutoExposureControl">Specify whether the exposure feature is controlled by relative or absolute values.
	   	
If specified as 'relative', exposure is controlled by the value specified by 
the AutoExposure property. If specified as 'absolute', exposure is controlled 
by the value specified by the AutoExposureAbsolute property.

<SeeAlso>AutoExposure.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>
	
	   	<AdaptorHelp property="Sharpness">Specify the image sharpness using relative values.

Sharpness specifies the subjective impression of the amount of detailed 
information provided in a picture image. When sharpness is controlled using 
relative values, the unit for sharpness is device specific. For more 
information about controlling the sharpness feature of a particular camera, 
see the documentation that came with the device.

Depending on the acquisition device, this property may have an associated 
SharpnessMode, SharpnessAbsolute, or SharpnessControl property.

Setting the Sharpness property will set the SharpnessMode property, if 
available, to 'manual' and the SharpnessControl property, if available, to 
'relative'.

<SeeAlso>SharpnessAbsolute, SharpnessControl, SharpnessMode.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>
	
	   	<AdaptorHelp property="SharpnessMode">Specify whether the Sharpness property is controlled manually or automatically.

If specified as 'auto', the device manages the sharpness setting. If specified 
as 'manual', you can control sharpness using the Sharpness or the 
SharpnessAbsolute property, if available.

The Sharpness, SharpnessAbsolute, SharpnessControl, and SharpnessMode 
properties are closely related. When you specify a value for either the 
Sharpness or SharpnessAbsolute property, the SharpnessMode property is 
automatically set to 'manual'. 

If you change the SharpnessMode property from 'auto' to 'manual', the 
Sharpness property is automatically set back to the value stored by that 
property if the SharpnessControl value is 'relative'. Otherwise, the 
SharpnessAbsolute property is automatically set back to the value stored by 
that property if the SharpnessControl value is 'absolute'.

<SeeAlso>Sharpness.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>

	   	<AdaptorHelp property="SharpnessAbsolute">Specify the sharpness using absolute values.
	   	
SharpnessAbsolute specifies the subjective impression of the amount of 
detailed information provided in a picture image. For more information about 
controlling the sharpness feature of a particular camera, see the 
documentation that came with the device.

Depending on the acquisition device, this property may have an associated mode 
property allowing this value to be controlled automatically by the device or 
for it to be manually configured.

Setting the SharpnessAbsolute property will set the SharpnessMode property, if 
available, to 'manual' and the SharpnessControl property to 'absolute'.

<SeeAlso>Sharpness, SharpnessMode, SharpnessControl.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>

	   	<AdaptorHelp property="SharpnessControl">Specify whether the sharpness feature is controlled by relative or absolute values.
	   	
If specified as 'relative', sharpness is controlled by the value specified by 
the Sharpness property. If specified as 'absolute', sharpness is controlled by 
the value specified by the SharpnessAbsolute property.

<SeeAlso>Sharpness.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>
	
	   	<AdaptorHelp property="WhiteBalance">Specify the color temperature of white using relative values.
	   	
The term "white balance" describes how a digital camera adjusts the color 
balance to compensate for the ambient lighting. You specify WhiteBalance as a 
vector containing two values. In YUV video format, WhiteBalance specifies U 
and V values. In RGB video format, WhiteBalance specifies B and R values. For 
more information about specifying the white balance on a particular camera, 
see the documentation that came with the device.

Depending on the acquisition device, this property may have an associated 
WhiteBalanceMode, WhiteBalanceAbsolute, or WhiteBalanceControl property.

Setting the WhiteBalance property will set the WhiteBalanceMode property, if 
available, to 'manual' and the WhiteBalanceControl property, if available, to 
'relative'.

<SeeAlso>WhiteBalanceAbsolute, WhiteBalanceControl, WhiteBalanceMode.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>

	   	<AdaptorHelp property="WhiteBalanceMode">Specify whether the WhiteBalance property is controlled manually or automatically.

If specified as 'auto', the device manages the white balance adjustment. If 
specified as 'manual', you can control white balance by using the WhiteBalance 
or the WhiteBalanceAbsolute property, if available.

The WhiteBalance, WhiteBalanceAbsolute, WhiteBalanceControl, and 
WhiteBalanceMode properties are closely related. When you specify a value for 
either the WhiteBalance or WhiteBalanceAbsolute property, the WhiteBalanceMode 
property is automatically set to 'manual'. 

If you change the WhiteBalanceMode property from 'auto' to 'manual', the 
WhiteBalance property is automatically set back to the value stored by that 
property if the WhiteBalanceControl value is 'relative'. Otherwise, the 
WhiteBalanceAbsolute property is automatically set back to the value stored by 
that property if the WhiteBalanceControl value is 'absolute'.

<SeeAlso>WhiteBalance.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>
	
	   	<AdaptorHelp property="WhiteBalanceAbsolute">Specify the color temperature of white using absolute values.
	   	
The term "white balance" describes how a digital camera adjusts the color 
balance to compensate for the ambient lighting. Using the WhiteBalanceAbsolute 
property, you can specify the color temperature (in Kelvin) of the white value 
the camera uses to calibrate colors. For more information about specifying the 
white balance on a particular camera, see the documentation that came with the 
device.

Depending on the acquisition device, this property may have an associated mode 
property allowing this value to be controlled automatically by the device or 
for it to be manually configured.

Setting the WhiteBalanceAbsolute property will set the WhiteBalanceMode 
property, if available, to 'manual' and the WhiteBalanceControl property to 
'absolute'.

<SeeAlso>WhiteBalance, WhiteBalanceMode, WhiteBalanceControl.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>

	   	<AdaptorHelp property="WhiteBalanceControl">Specify whether the white balance feature is controlled by relative or absolute values.
	   	
If specified as 'relative', white balance is controlled by the value specified 
by the WhiteBalance property. If specified as 'absolute', white balance is 
controlled by the value specified by the WhiteBalanceAbsolute property.

<SeeAlso>WhiteBalance.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>

	   	<AdaptorHelp property="Hue">Specify color on color circle using relative values.
	   	 
Hue specifies color as an angle on the color circle. When hue is controlled 
using relative values, the unit for hue is device specific. For more 
information about controlling the hue feature of a particular camera, see the 
documentation that came with the device.

Depending on the acquisition device, this property may have an associated 
HueMode, HueAbsolute, or HueControl property.

Setting the Hue property will set the HueMode property, if available, to 
'manual' and the HueControl property, if available, to 'relative'.

<SeeAlso>HueAbsolute, HueControl, HueMode.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>

	   	<AdaptorHelp property="HueMode">Specify whether the Hue property is controlled manually or automatically.

If specified as 'auto', the device manages the hue value. If specified as 
'manual', you can control hue by using the Hue or the HueAbsolute property, if 
available. 

The Hue, HueAbsolute, HueControl, and HueMode properties are closely related. 
When you specify a value for either the Hue or HueAbsolute property, the 
HueMode property is automatically set to 'manual'.

If you change the HueMode property from 'auto' to 'manual', the Hue property 
is automatically set back to the value stored by that property if the 
HueControl value is 'relative'. Otherwise, HueAbsolute property is 
automatically set back to the value stored by that property if the HueControl 
value is 'absolute'.

<SeeAlso>Hue.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>
	
	   	<AdaptorHelp property="HueAbsolute">Specify color on color circle using absolute values.
	  
Hue specifies color as an angle on the color circle. You specify the angle in 
degrees. Positive values specify an angle counterclockwise from the origin; 
negative values specify angles clockwise from the origin. For more information 
on specifying Hue with a particular camera, see the documentation that came 
with the device.
	  
Depending on the acquisition device, this property may have an associated mode 
property allowing this value to be controlled automatically by the device or 
for it to be manually configured.

Setting the HueAbsolute property will set the HueMode property, if available, 
to 'manual' and the HueControl property to 'absolute'.

<SeeAlso>Hue, HueMode, HueControl.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>

	   	<AdaptorHelp property="HueControl">Specify whether the hue feature is controlled by relative or absolute values.
	   	
If specified as 'relative', hue is controlled by the value specified by the 
Hue property. If specified as 'absolute', hue is controlled by the value 
specified by the HueAbsolute property.

<SeeAlso>Hue.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>
	
	   	<AdaptorHelp property="Saturation">Specify the intensity of colors using relative values.

Saturation specifies the intensity of the colors. Intensity is determined by 
the amount of gray in a color. A color with more gray is considered less 
saturated; a color with more gray is considered more saturated. When 
saturation is controlled using relative values, the unit for saturation is 
device specific. For more information about controlling the color saturation 
of a particular camera, see the documentation that came with the device.

Depending on the acquisition device, this property may have an associated 
SaturationMode, SaturationAbsolute, or SaturationControl property.

Setting the Saturation property will set the SaturationMode property, if 
available, to 'manual' and the SaturationControl property, if available, to 
'relative'.

<SeeAlso>SaturationAbsolute, SaturationControl, SaturationMode.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>
	
	   	<AdaptorHelp property="SaturationMode">Specify whether the Saturation property is controlled manually or automatically.

If specified as 'auto', the device manages the saturation value. If specified 
as 'manual', you can control saturation by using the Saturation or the 
SaturationAbsolute property, if available.

The Saturation, SaturationAbsolute, SaturationControl, and SaturationMode 
properties are closely related. When you specify a value for either the 
Saturation or SaturationAbsolute property, the SaturationMode property is 
automatically set to 'manual'. 

If you change the SaturationMode property from 'auto' to 'manual', the 
Saturation property is automatically set back to the value stored by that 
property if the SaturationControl value is 'relative'. Otherwise, the 
SaturationAbsolute property is automatically set back to the value stored by 
that property if the SaturationControl value is 'absolute'.

<SeeAlso>Saturation.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>
	
	   	<AdaptorHelp property="SaturationAbsolute">Specify the intensity of colors using absolute values.

Saturation specifies the intensity of the colors. Intensity is determined by 
the amount of gray in a color. A color with more gray is considered less 
saturated; a color with more gray is considered more saturated. You specify 
the saturation level as a percentage with reference to the original image at 
100 percent. For more information about controlling the color saturation of a 
particular camera, see the documentation that came with the device.

Depending on the acquisition device, this property may have an associated mode 
property allowing this value to be controlled automatically by the device or 
for it to be manually configured.

Setting the SaturationAbsolute property will set the SaturationMode property, 
if available, to 'manual' and the SaturationControl property to 'absolute'.

<SeeAlso>Saturation, SaturationMode, SaturationControl.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>

	   	<AdaptorHelp property="SaturationControl">Specify whether the saturation feature is controlled by relative or absolute values.
	   	
If specified as 'relative', saturation is controlled by the value specified by 
the Saturation property. If specified as 'absolute', saturation is controlled 
by the value specified by the SaturationAbsolute property.

<SeeAlso>Saturation.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>

	   	<AdaptorHelp property="Gamma">Specify the gamma level using relative values.
	   	
The gamma defines the function between the light level input and the light 
level picture output. When gamma is controlled using relative values, the unit 
for gamma is device specific. For more information on the light level relation 
function and the Gamma value, see the documentation that came with the device.

Depending on the acquisition device, this property may have an associated 
GammaMode, GammaAbsolute, or GammaControl property.

Setting the Gamma property will set the GammaMode property, if available, to 
'manual' and the GammaControl property, if available, to 'relative'.
   	
<SeeAlso>GammaAbsolute, GammaControl, GammaMode.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>

	   	<AdaptorHelp property="GammaMode">Specify whether the Gamma property is controlled manually or automatically.

If specified as 'auto', the device manages the gamma value. If specified as 
'manual', you can control gamma by using the Gamma or the GammaAbsolute 
property, if available.

The Gamma, GammaAbsolute, GammaControl, and GammaMode properties are closely 
related. When you specify a value for either the Gamma or GammaAbsolute 
property, the GammaMode property is automatically set to 'manual'.

If you change the GammaMode property from 'auto' to 'manual', the Gamma 
property is automatically set back to the value stored by that property if the 
GammaControl value is 'relative'. Otherwise, the GammaAbsolute property is 
automatically set back to the value stored by that property if the 
GammaControl value is 'absolute'.

<SeeAlso>Gamma.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>
	
	   	<AdaptorHelp property="GammaAbsolute">Specify the gamma level using absolute values.

The gamma defines the function between the light level input and the light 
level picture output. For more information on the light level relation 
function and the Gamma value, see the documentation that came with the device.

Depending on the acquisition device, this property may have an associated mode 
property allowing this value to be controlled automatically by the device or 
for it to be manually configured.

Setting the GammaAbsolute property will set the GammaMode property, if 
available, to 'manual' and the GammaControl property to 'absolute'.

<SeeAlso>Gamma, GammaMode, GammaControl.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>

	   	<AdaptorHelp property="GammaControl">Specify whether the gamma feature is controlled by relative or absolute values.
	   	
If specified as 'relative', gamma is controlled by the value specified by the 
Gamma property. If specified as 'absolute', gamma is controlled by the value 
specified by the GammaAbsolute property.

<SeeAlso>Gamma.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>

	   	<AdaptorHelp property="Shutter">Specify the amount of time that the sensor is exposed to light using relative values.
	   	
The Shutter property specifies the amount of time that the sensor is exposed 
to light, also known as integration time. When shutter is controlled using 
relative values, the unit for shutter is device specific. For more information 
about setting the shutter speed of a particular camera, see the documentation 
that came with the device.

Depending on the acquisition device, this property may have an associated 
ShutterMode, ShutterAbsolute, or ShutterControl property.

Setting the Shutter property will set the ShutterMode property, if available, 
to 'manual' and the ShutterControl property, if available, to 'relative'.

<SeeAlso>ShutterAbsolute, ShutterControl, ShutterMode.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>
	
	   	<AdaptorHelp property="ShutterMode">Specify whether the Shutter property is controlled manually or automatically.

If specified as 'auto', the device manages the shutter value. If specified as 
'manual', you can control shutter by using the Shutter or the ShutterAbsolute 
property, if available. 

The Shutter, ShutterAbsolute, ShutterControl, and ShutterMode properties are 
closely related. When you specify a value for either the Shutter or 
ShutterAbsolute property, the ShutterMode property is automatically set to 
'manual'.

If you change the ShutterMode property from 'auto' to 'manual', the Shutter 
property is automatically set back to the value stored by that property if the 
ShutterControl value is 'relative'. Otherwise, the ShutterAbsolute property is 
automatically set back to the value stored by that property if the 
ShutterControl value is 'absolute'.

<SeeAlso>Shutter.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>
	
	   	<AdaptorHelp property="ShutterAbsolute">Specify the amount of time that the sensor is exposed to light using absolute values.
	   	
The Shutter property specifies the amount of time that the sensor is exposed 
to light, also known as integration time. You specify the shutter speed in 
seconds. For more information about setting the shutter speed of a particular 
camera, see the documentation that came with the device.

Depending on the acquisition device, this property may have an associated mode 
property allowing this value to be controlled automatically by the device or 
for it to be manually configured.

Setting the ShutterAbsolute property will set the ShutterMode property, if 
available, to 'manual' and the ShutterControl property to 'absolute'.

<SeeAlso>Shutter, ShutterMode, ShutterControl.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>

	   	<AdaptorHelp property="ShutterControl">Specify whether the shutter feature is controlled by relative or absolute values.
	   	
If specified as 'relative', shutter is controlled by the value specified by 
the Shutter property. If specified as 'absolute', shutter is controlled by the 
value specified by the ShutterAbsolute property.

<SeeAlso>Shutter.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>
	
	   	<AdaptorHelp property="Gain">Specify the gain adjustment using relative values.

Gain describes the circuit gain on the original image. When gain is controlled 
using relative values, the unit for gain is device specific. For more 
information about controlling gain in a particular camera, see the documention 
that came with the device.

Depending on the acquisition device, this property may have an associated 
GainMode, GainAbsolute, or GainControl property.

Setting the Gain property will set the GainMode property, if available, to 
'manual' and the GainControl property, if available, to 'relative'.

<SeeAlso>GainAbsolute, GainControl, GainMode.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>
	
	   	<AdaptorHelp property="GainMode">Specify whether the Gain property is controlled manually or automatically.

If specified as 'auto', the device manages the gain value. If specified as 
'manual', you can control gain by using the Gain or the GainAbsolute property, 
if available.

The Gain, GainAbsolute, GainControl, and GainMode properties are closely 
related. When you specify a value for either the Gain or GainAbsolute 
property, the GainMode property is automatically set to 'manual'. 

If you change the GainMode property from 'auto' to 'manual', the Gain property 
is automatically set back to the value stored by that property if the 
GainControl value is 'relative'. Otherwise, the GainAbsolute property is 
automatically set back to the value stored by that property if the GainControl 
value is 'absolute'.

<SeeAlso>Gain.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>
	
	   	<AdaptorHelp property="GainAbsolute">Specify the gain adjustment using absolute values.
	   	
Gain describes the circuit gain in decibels (dB) with reference to the 
original image at 0. For more information about setting the gain adjustment 
for a particular camera, see the documentation that came with the device.

Depending on the acquisition device, this property may have an associated mode 
property allowing this value to be controlled automatically by the device or 
for it to be manually configured.

Setting the GainAbsolute property will set the GainMode property, if 
available, to 'manual' and the GainControl property to 'absolute'.

<SeeAlso>Gain, GainMode, GainControl.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>

	   	<AdaptorHelp property="GainControl">Specify whether the gain feature is controlled by relative or absolute values.
	   	
If specified as 'relative', gain is controlled by the value specified by the 
Gain property. If specified as 'absolute', gain is controlled by the value 
specified by the GainAbsolute property.

<SeeAlso>Gain.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>
	
	   	<AdaptorHelp property="Iris">Specify the camera aperture opening using relative values.

A camera's iris (or aperture) is the opening through which light enters the 
camera. Camera's provide a mechanism through which you can control the size of 
this opening. When iris is controlled using relative values, the unit for iris 
is device specific. For more information about controlling the iris of a 
particular camera, see the documentation that came with the device.

Depending on the acquisition device, this property may have an associated 
IrisMode, IrisAbsolute, or IrisControl property.

Setting the Iris property will set the IrisMode property, if available, to 
'manual' and the IrisControl property, if available, to 'relative'.

<SeeAlso>IrisAbsolute, IrisControl, IrisMode.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>
	
	   	<AdaptorHelp property="IrisMode">Specify whether the Iris property is controlled manually or automatically.

If specified as 'auto', the device manages the iris value. If specified as 
'manual', you can control iris by using the Iris or the IrisAbsolute property, 
if available.

The Iris, IrisAbsolute, IrisControl, and IrisMode properties are closely 
related. When you specify a value for either the Iris or IrisAbsolute 
property, the IrisMode property is automatically set to 'manual'. 

If you change the IrisMode property from 'auto' to 'manual', the Iris property 
is automatically set back to the value stored by that property if the 
IrisControl value is 'relative'. Otherwise, the IrisAbsolute property is 
automatically set back to the value stored by that property if the IrisControl 
value is 'absolute'.

<SeeAlso>Iris.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>
	
	   	<AdaptorHelp property="IrisAbsolute">Specify the camera aperture opening using absolute values.
	   	
A camera's iris (or aperture) is the opening through which light enters the 
camera. Camera's provide a mechanism through which you can control the size of 
this opening. Using the IrisAbsolute property, you can specify the size as an 
F number. For more information about controlling the iris of a particular 
camera, see the documentation that came with the device.
	  
Depending on the acquisition device, this property may have an associated mode 
property allowing this value to be controlled automatically by the device or 
for it to be manually configured.

Setting the IrisAbsolute property will set the IrisMode property, if 
available, to 'manual' and the IrisControl property to 'absolute'.

<SeeAlso>Iris, IrisMode, IrisControl.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>

	   	<AdaptorHelp property="IrisControl">Specify whether the iris feature is controlled by relative or absolute values.
	   	
If specified as 'relative', aperture is controlled by the value specified by 
the Iris property. If specified as 'absolute', aperture is controlled by the 
value specified by the IrisAbsolute property.

<SeeAlso>Iris.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>

	   	<AdaptorHelp property="Focus">Specify the camera-to-subject distance using relative values.

Focus is the process of adjusting the distance setting on a camera's lens 
system to produce a sharp image. The Focus property specifies the 
camera-to-subject distance. When focus is controlled using relative values, 
the unit for focal distance is device specific. For more information about the 
focusing features of a specific camera, see the documentation that came with 
the device.

Depending on the acquisition device, this property may have an associated 
FocusMode, FocusAbsolute, or FocusControl property.

Setting the Focus property will set the FocusMode property, if available, to 
'manual' and the FocusControl property, if available, to 'relative'.

<SeeAlso>FocusAbsolute, FocusControl, FocusMode.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>

	   	<AdaptorHelp property="FocusMode">Specify whether the Focus property is controlled manually or automatically.

If specified as 'auto', the device manages the focus value. If specified as 
'manual', you can control focus by using the Focus or the FocusAbsolute 
property, if available.

The Focus, FocusAbsolute, FocusControl, and FocusMode properties are closely 
related. When you specify a value for either the Focus or FocusAbsolute 
property, the FocusMode property is automatically set to 'manual'. 

If you change the FocusMode property from 'auto' to 'manual', the Focus 
property is automatically set back to the value stored by that property if the 
FocusControl value is 'relative'. Otherwise, the FocusAbsolute property is 
automatically set back to the value stored by that property if the 
FocusControl value is 'absolute'.

<SeeAlso>Focus.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>
	
	   	<AdaptorHelp property="FocusAbsolute">Specify the camera-to-subject distance using absolute values.
	   	
FocusAbsolute is the process of adjusting the distance setting on a camera's 
lens system to produce a sharp image. The FocusAbsolute property specifies the 
camera-to-subject distance in meters. For more information about the focusing 
features of a specific camera, see the documentation that came with the device.
 
Depending on the acquisition device, this property may have an associated mode 
property allowing this value to be controlled automatically by the device or 
for it to be manually configured.

Setting the FocusAbsolute property will set the FocusMode property, if 
available, to 'manual' and the FocusControl property to 'absolute'.

<SeeAlso>Focus, FocusMode, FocusControl.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>

	   	<AdaptorHelp property="FocusControl">Specify whether the focus feature is controlled by relative or absolute values.
	   	
If specified as 'relative', focus is controlled by the value specified by the 
Focus property. If specified as 'absolute', focus is controlled by the value 
specified by the FocusAbsolute property.

<SeeAlso>Focus.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>
	
	   	<AdaptorHelp property="Temperature">Specify target temperature for the device using relative values.

Temperature specifies the target temperature at which you want the camera to 
operate. For more information about the temperature capabilities of a 
particular camera, see the documentation that came with the device.

Depending on the acquisition device, this property may have an associated 
TemperatureMode, TemperatureAbsolute, or TemperatureControl property.

Setting the Temperature property will set the TemperatureMode property, if 
available, to 'manual' and the TemperatureControl property, if available, to 
'relative'.

<SeeAlso>TemperatureAbsolute, TemperatureControl, TemperatureMode.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>
	
	   	<AdaptorHelp property="TemperatureMode">Specify whether the Temperature property is controlled manually or automatically.

If specified as 'auto', the device manages the target temperature value. If 
specified as 'manual', you can control the target temperature by using the 
Temperature or the TemperatureAbsolute property, if available. 

The Temperature, TemperatureAbsolute, TemperatureControl, and TemperatureMode 
properties are closely related. When you specify a value for either the 
Temperature or TemperatureAbsolute property, the TemperatureMode property is 
automatically set to 'manual'. 

If you change the TemperatureMode property from 'auto' to 'manual', the 
Temperature property is automatically set back to the value stored by that 
property if the TemperatureControl value is 'relative'. Otherwise, the 
TemperatureAbsolute property is automatically set back to the value stored by 
that property if the TemperatureControl value is 'absolute'.

<SeeAlso>Temperature.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>
	
	   	<AdaptorHelp property="TemperatureAbsolute">Specify target temperature for the device using absolute values.
	   	
TemperatureAbsolute specifies the target temperature at which you want the 
camera to operate. For more information about the temperature capabilities of 
a particular camera, see the documentation that came with the device.

Depending on the acquisition device, this property may have an associated mode 
property allowing this value to be controlled automatically by the device or 
for it to be manually configured.

Setting the TemperatureAbsolute property will set the TemperatureMode 
property, if available, to 'manual' and the TemperatureControl property to 
'absolute'.

<SeeAlso>Temperature, TemperatureMode, TemperatureControl.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>

	   	<AdaptorHelp property="TemperatureControl">Specify whether the temperature feature is controlled by relative or absolute values.
	   	
If specified as 'relative', target temperature is controlled by the value 
specified by the Temperature property. If specified as 'absolute', target 
temperature is controlled by the value specified by the TemperatureAbsolute 
property.

<SeeAlso>Temperature.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>
	
	   	<AdaptorHelp property="Zoom">Specify the lens magnification using relative values.

Zoom describes the lens magnification. When lens magnification is controlled 
using relative values, the unit of magnification is device specific. For more 
information on the lens magnification of a particular camera, see the 
documentation that came with the device. 

Depending on the acquisition device, this property may have an associated 
ZoomMode, ZoomAbsolute, or ZoomControl property.

Setting the Zoom property will set the ZoomMode property, if available, to 
'manual' and the ZoomControl property, if available, to 'relative'.

<SeeAlso>ZoomAbsolute, ZoomControl, ZoomMode.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>

	   	<AdaptorHelp property="ZoomMode">Specify whether the Zoom property is controlled manually or automatically.

If specified as 'auto', the device manages the zoom value. If specified as 
'manual', you can control zoom by using the Zoom or the ZoomAbsolute property, 
if available.

The Zoom, ZoomAbsolute, ZoomControl, and ZoomMode properties are closely 
related. When you specify a value for either the Zoom or ZoomAbsolute 
property, the ZoomMode property is automatically set to 'manual'. 

If you change the ZoomMode property from 'auto' to 'manual', the Zoom 
property is automatically set back to the value stored by that property if 
the ZoomControl value is 'relative'. Otherwise, the ZoomAbsolute property is 
automatically set back to the value stored by that property if the ZoomControl 
value is 'absolute'.

<SeeAlso>Zoom.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>
	
	   	<AdaptorHelp property="ZoomAbsolute">Specify the lens magnification using absolute values.
	   	
ZoomAbsolute describes the lens magnification in power relative to the 
original image. For more information on the magnification power of a 
particular camera, see the documentation that came with the device. 
	   	
Depending on the acquisition device, this property may have an associated mode 
property allowing this value to be controlled automatically by the device or 
for it to be manually configured.

Setting the ZoomAbsolute property will set the ZoomMode property, if 
available, to 'manual' and the ZoomControl property to 'absolute'.

<SeeAlso>Zoom, ZoomMode, ZoomControl.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>

	   	<AdaptorHelp property="ZoomControl">Specify whether the zoom feature is controlled by relative or absolute values.
	   	
If specified as 'relative', zoom is controlled by the value specified by the 
Zoom property. If specified as 'absolute', zoom is controlled by the value 
specified by the ZoomAbsolute property.

<SeeAlso>Zoom.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>
	
	   	<AdaptorHelp property="Pan">Specify camera horizontal positioning using relative values.

Pan specifies the horizontal positioning of a camera. When pan is controlled 
using relative values, the unit for horizontal positioning is device specific. 
For more information controlling the panning of a particular camera, see the 
documentation that came with the device. 

Depending on the acquisition device, this property may have an associated 
PanMode, PanAbsolute, or PanControl property.

Setting the Pan property will set the PanMode property, if available, to 
'manual' and the PanControl property, if available, to 'relative'.

<SeeAlso>PanAbsolute, PanControl, PanMode.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>
	
	   	<AdaptorHelp property="PanMode">Specify whether the Pan property is controlled manually or automatically.

If specified as 'auto', the device manages the pan value. If specified as 
'manual', you can control pan by using the Pan or the PanAbsolute property, if 
available.

The Pan, PanAbsolute, PanControl, and PanMode properties are closely related. 
When you specify a value for either the Pan or PanAbsolute property, the 
PanMode property is automatically set to 'manual'. 

If you change the PanMode property from 'auto' to 'manual', the Pan property 
is automatically set back to the value stored by that property if the 
PanControl value is 'relative'. Otherwise, the PanAbsolute property is 
automatically set back to the value stored by that property if the PanControl 
value is 'absolute'.

<SeeAlso>Pan.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>
	
	   	<AdaptorHelp property="PanAbsolute">Specify camera horizontal positioning using absolute values.
	   	
PanAbsolute specifies the horizontal positioning of a camera in degrees. 
Positive values indicate clockwise movement from the origin (the camera 
rotates clockwise when viewed from above); negative values indicate 
counterclockwise movement from the origin. For more information controlling 
the panning of a particular camera, see the documentation that came with the 
device. 

Depending on the acquisition device, this property may have an associated mode 
property allowing this value to be controlled automatically by the device or 
for it to be manually configured.

Setting the PanAbsolute property will set the PanMode property, if available, 
to 'manual' and the PanControl property to 'absolute'.

<SeeAlso>Pan, PanMode, PanControl.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>

	   	<AdaptorHelp property="PanControl">Specify whether the pan feature is controlled by relative or absolute values.
	   	
If specified as 'relative', pan is controlled by the value specified by the 
Pan property. If specified as 'absolute', pan is controlled by the value 
specified by the PanAbsolute property.

<SeeAlso>Pan.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>
	
	   	<AdaptorHelp property="Tilt">Specify camera vertical positioning using relative values.

Tilt specifies the vertical positioning of a camera. When Tilt is controlled 
using relative values, the unit for vertical positioning is device specific. 
For more information controlling the tilt of a particular camera, see the 
documentation that came with the device. 

Depending on the acquisition device, this property may have an associated 
TiltMode, TiltAbsolute, or TiltControl property.

Setting the Tilt property will set the TiltMode property, if available, to 
'manual' and the TiltControl property, if available, to 'relative'.

<SeeAlso>TiltAbsolute, TiltControl, TiltMode.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>
	
	   	<AdaptorHelp property="TiltMode">Specify whether the Tilt property is controlled manually or automatically.

If specified as 'auto', the device manages the tilt value. If specified as 
'manual', you can control tilt by using the Tilt or the TiltAbsolute property, 
if available.

The Tilt, TiltAbsolute, TiltControl, and TiltMode properties are closely 
related. When you specify a value for either the Tilt or TiltAbsolute 
property, the TiltMode property is automatically set to 'manual'. 

If you change the TiltMode property from 'auto' to 'manual', the Tilt property 
is automatically set back to the value stored by that property if the 
TiltControl value is 'relative'. Otherwise, the TiltAbsolute property is 
automatically set back to the value stored by that property if the TiltControl 
value is 'absolute'.

<SeeAlso>Tilt.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>
	
	   	<AdaptorHelp property="TiltAbsolute">Specify camera vertical positioning using absolute values.
	   	
TiltAbsolute specifies the vertical positioning of the camera in degrees. 
Positive values indicate upward movement from the origin; negative values 
indicate downward movement from the origin. For more information on 
controlling tilt in a particular camera, see the documentation that came with 
the device.

Depending on the acquisition device, this property may have an associated mode 
property allowing this value to be controlled automatically by the device or 
for it to be manually configured.

Setting the TiltAbsolute property will set the TiltMode property, if 
available, to 'manual' and the TiltControl property to 'absolute'.

<SeeAlso>Tilt, TiltMode, TiltControl.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>

	   	<AdaptorHelp property="TiltControl">Specify whether the tilt feature is controlled by relative or absolute values.
	   	
If specified as 'relative', tilt is controlled by the value specified by the 
Tilt property. If specified as 'absolute', tilt is controlled by the value 
specified by the TiltAbsolute property.

<SeeAlso>Tilt.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>
	
	   	<AdaptorHelp property="OpticalFilter">Specify the optical filter setting using relative values.

OpticalFilter describes the camera's optical lens filter function. For more 
information on the optical lens filter and the OpticalFilter value, see the 
documentation that came with the device. 

Depending on the acquisition device, this property may have an associated 
OpticalFilterMode, OpticalFilterAbsolute, or OpticalFilterControl property.

Setting the OpticalFilter property will set the OpticalFilterMode property, if 
available, to 'manual' and the OpticalFilterControl property, if available, to 
'relative'.

<SeeAlso>OpticalFilterAbsolute, OpticalFilterControl, OpticalFilterMode.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>
	
	   	<AdaptorHelp property="OpticalFilterMode">Specify whether the OpticalFilter property is controlled manually or automatically.

If specified as 'auto', the device manages the optical filter value. If 
specified as 'manual', you can control optical filter by using the 
OpticalFilter or the OpticalFilterAbsolute property, if available.

The OpticalFilter, OpticalFilterAbsolute, OpticalFilterControl, and 
OpticalFilterMode properties are closely related. When you specify a value for 
either the OpticalFilter or OpticalFilterAbsolute property, the 
OpticalFilterMode property is automatically set to 'manual'. 

If you change the OpticalFilterMode property from 'auto' to 'manual', the 
OpticalFilter property is automatically set back to the value stored by that 
property if the OpticalFilterControl value is 'relative'. Otherwise, the 
OpticalFilterAbsolute property is automatically set back to the value stored 
by that property if the OpticalFilterControl value is 'absolute'.

<SeeAlso>OpticalFilter.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>
	
	   	<AdaptorHelp property="OpticalFilterAbsolute">Specify the optical filter setting using absolute values.
	   	
OpticalFilter describes the camera's optical lens filter function. For more 
information on the optical lens filter and the OpticalFilter value, see the 
documentation that came with the device. 

Depending on the acquisition device, this property may have an associated mode 
property allowing this value to be controlled automatically by the device or 
for it to be manually configured.

Setting the OpticalFilterAbsolute property will set the OpticalFilterMode 
property, if available, to 'manual' and the OpticalFilterControl property to 
'absolute'.

<SeeAlso>OpticalFilter, OpticalFilterMode, OpticalFilterControl.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>

	   	<AdaptorHelp property="OpticalFilterControl">Specify whether the optical filter feature is controlled by relative or absolute values.
	   	
If specified as 'relative', lens filter is controlled by the value specified 
by the OpticalFilter property. If specified as 'absolute', lens filter is 
controlled by the value specified by the OpticalFilterAbsolute property.

<SeeAlso>OpticalFilter.</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>
	
	   	<AdaptorHelp property="FrameRate">Specify the device frame rate.

FrameRate determines the rate at which frames are acquired by the device. The 
FrameRate value is measured in number of frames per second.

</AdaptorHelp>

	</Help>
</ImageAcquisitionInfo>
